our best to find the answers for people  we're on the banks of the cape fear  river in historic wilmington north  carolina  coming up we'll sit down with one  wilmington author who writes about the  u.s government-led effort to save  thousands of vietnamese children before  the fall of saigon in 1975  operation baby lift was an evacuation  of  two to three thousand vietnamese  displaced children  from saigon at the very end of the war  there in  april of 1975. the purpose of the baby  lift was to remove from vietnam  these several thousand displaced  children  who  were considered  orphans and therefore in need of stable  homes somewhere else and this was right  at the time when um the war in vietnam  which had lasted for 30 years it had  basically basically been going on since  1945 in different forms  was about to end so in march of 1975 the  north vietnamese began their their final  move into south vietnam and within six  weeks this 30-year war  really quickly came to an end and  there were a number of uh  small  and larger adoption  agencies in vietnam that were working  with children and preparing them for  adoption and these agencies decided at  that point when the north vietnamese was  about to take over that they needed to  expedite those adoptions and get the  children in their care out of vietnam as  quickly as possible and so with the help  of the us government they arranged an  airlift and they evacuated these  children the united states  left um  left vietnam or pulled out for the most  part in the early 1970s but it took a  while for  um it was the the  the war was still kind of at a stalemate  and so it wasn't until march of 1975  that the  that the north finally began to make the  moves that ended up  bringing the war to an end and that was  by  um moving south  starting in the central highlands and  then sort of slowly moving continuously  south  um from there and the south vietnamese  government made a really bad choice to  kind of pull back when the north began  this this final kind of invasion and  pull back and and try to fight in the  area where they felt most comfortable  and what happened was that caused a  panic and so people in the northern part  of south vietnam  felt that they had been abandoned by the  south vietnamese government and  they started fleeing farther south and  so you had you had um hundreds of  thousands of people  fleeing one part of the country and  moving south toward towards the  east and south so toward the coast and  then south towards saigon  and i guess the best way to describe  what was going on in that last six weeks  of the war was increasing chaos and  panic and a lot of south vietnamese  wanting to get out of the country as  well because they were afraid of what  was going to happen when the communists  took over  that point and um there was something  like  800 000  displaced children in vietnam  so  that's a really really large number and  that was a result to a large extent of  the war and by displaced i don't  necessarily mean that they were orphans  but that they were not able to be raised  by their parents  when it became clear that the south  vietnamese government was going to  collapse  the  adoption agency administrators in saigon  understood that this was going to be a  crisis for their agencies and for the  children that they were trying to send  for adoption overseas and many of these  children had been in the adoption  pipeline as they called it for months or  years already and they were just waiting  because adoption generally can take a  long time to process  so they became concerned that the  children who were in their care were not  going to end up being able to go  overseas because the government would  collapse and that they would have to  leave them in vietnam and they were  worried about their  what would happen to them and so they  began to  appeal to the u.s government to help  to  um  remove these children from the country  and it took a while and it took um there  was a there was um a man named ed daley  who ran a his own private airlines i  mean he  he was his airline was called world  airways and he he  brought in a lot of military equipment  into vietnam um so he made a lot of  money in vietnam but he also  had some concern about the children so  he ended up flying up the very first  plane load of children who left vietnam  on operation baby lift were actually  airlifted out on one of his planes i  mean it was you could say it was a big  publicity stunt you could say that he  was doing it because he um was really  concerned about these children for  whatever reason he airlifted the first  group and the us government seeing that  he had done this on his own and that it  was done in a way that was not  legally proper  decided to get involved and with that  gerald ford  decided to begin a more organized  evacuation  that was eventually called  operation baby lift or the orphan  airlift  uh the children who were being adopted  were for the most part going to the  united states although a significant  number went to europe and also australia  um  many if not most of them were already  scheduled to be adopted by families who  had been going through this process for  a while already  but in the end especially in the last  few weeks um i mean anybody who's seen  the photographs of the very end of the  war the fall of saigon and the the  helicopter on top of the u.s government  building  people climbing up to get in and just  the complete chaos at that time knows  that nothing worked in a really  organized and efficient manner and one  of the  results of that in terms of the baby  lift was that a lot of parents at the  very end of the war vietnamese parents  were so panicked about what was going to  happen to their children  after the communists took over that they  did whatever they could to get children  get their children into these adoption  agencies and on these planes to be  adopted overseas even if it meant giving  up those children and their ability to  care for them and so a lot of a lot of  the children  maybe  maybe  10 or 20 so not a majority but a  significant number um  sort of ended up on these planes at the  last minute and they were not already in  the adoption pipeline and then by the  time they got to the u.s um or the  countries that they ended up in there  was a question of where are we gonna how  how are we gonna place these peop these  children and where will we put them that  sort of thing first  u.s  sponsored plane that that was  uh  dedicated towards the baby lift was a  galaxy  aircraft which is a military aircraft  and it it came in to  vietnam on april 4th  and children were very quickly loaded  into the plane i think there were 200  and something children loaded onto a big  um cargo jet so i mean you know what the  inside of a passenger plane looks like  it's got  seats on it but this was a cargo jet so  most of it um the bottom part of it was  like the inside of a warehouse so  there's no seats there's there's no way  to sort of protect anybody that's on  there but it was it was such a  moment of chaos and and and concern  about what was going to happen that all  the children were just put onto the  plane very quickly so there were many  older children on the bottom of the  plane in this cargo area plus the  caregivers who got on with them and then  in the top part of the plane which is  kind of a passenger area there were a  lot of infants and nurses and people who  were taking care of the sickest children  so a few minutes after that plane took  off  the rear cargo door blew off  there was a big explosion  and  the pilots were able to maintain some  control over the plane they turned the  plane around  there's there are  testimonies from from people who were on  the top part of the plane who tell us  what happened what it was like inside  the plane um the fear that they felt  they were out over the water over the  ocean at that time the pilots were  incredible and they were able to turn  the plane around and try to get back to  saigon but they couldn't make it back to  saigon and the plane crashed in the rice  fields outside of saigon  we don't even know how many people were  killed because we don't know how many  people were actually on the plane as i  said everything was done in such a  disorganized rushed way  that and then a lot of the papers were  destroyed that it's hard to know there  were children who survived up above but  on the in the cargo area i think almost  everybody was killed  so that was um  it was like one heartbreak following so  many years of heartbreak already  and  so there was a question briefly about  whether or not the baby lift would  continue  but the president gerald ford said  we're going to keep going operation baby  lift lasted from  um for about three weeks  in maybe i should say a month in in  april of 1975 and when saigon fell  there were no more planes leaving  leaving vietnam with children in them so  it was a very short time in a very  intense time and during that period  something between two and three thousand  children were evacuated  following the baby lift  um and and the fall of saigon in the  months that the months after that  a group of lawyers filed a class action  lawsuit on behalf of these children  just to slow down the process of the  adoptions and make sure that all the  children who were being adopted were  legally eligible for adoption  um under the terms of the geneva accords  and um  that lawsuit was eventually thrown out  because um  more for legal reasons than on the  merits of the case because um the judge  decided that that these these children  didn't serve as a class which a  class-action lawsuit everybody has to  have suffered in the same way  anyway  there were individual lawsuits though  that that did go through and these were  mostly um  based on  birth parents who gave up their children  at the very end of the war mostly birth  mothers  and then they managed to get out of  vietnam themselves and get to the united  states as refugees and when they got to  the united states they looked for their  children and they when they found them  there were certain situations where the  adoptive families had had the children  for months or maybe even a year and the  birth parents filed to  regain custody of their children saying  that they had to give them up under  duress and those lawsuits played out and  a number of those there were there was  maybe less than 10 i think  those lawsuits generally ended with the  birth parents  receiving custody of their children  again but they were very complicated and  difficult for the most part adoptive  families ended up raising these children  i actually been doing research for a new  book on the refugee situation in  in europe and the middle east and it's  been very interesting to  be learning about that in the context of  what happened during operation baby lift  because there is very very little talk  about adoption now  the  the discussion of refugees focuses on  keeping families together  if there are orphans and there are  orphans unfortunately  there seem to be  real serious efforts to find them family  somewhere  a lot of times these families who are  fleeing  syria for example already have some  family members who are living in europe  and so a lot of times those orphans will  end up going to live with aunts and  uncles and cousins and grandparents  in countries in europe so i think that  i'm not saying that they're directly  thinking of operation baby lift when  they think about this policy but i think  that a lot has changed in the decades  since the baby lift and that  public policy makers and humanitarian  agencies and probably adoption agencies  as well are making an effort to really  do their homework and  not be pressed by chaos to rush into  anything even there was a um  there was an earthquake in haiti a few  years ago and there were some children  there  who were  in the process of being adopted by  american families and those children  because the situation in haiti was so  bad those children were airlifted out of  haiti and brought to i think  pennsylvania and  the adoption agency made some effort to  just expedite the adoption so that the  children could go into the families that  they were supposed to be adopted  um with  quicker  and the u.s government said no we are  not speeding up the process of adoption  just because of what happened there's  still things that need to be done to  make sure  that this is appropriate and i think  that's great  i want people who read this book  to understand that  these issues of what happens to  children and their families are really  complicated they are they can't be  solved easily  but that with time we can get there and  we need to do that for the children so  that we don't make mistakes that can't  really be remedied later  c-span where history unfold

